<h3 id="4-when-drawrect--layoutsubviews-get-called">4. When drawrect &amp; layoutsubviews get called</h3>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">layoutSubviews:</code> (equivalent to the layoutSubviews() function) is called in the following situations:</p>

<ol>
  <li>init does not trigger layoutSubviews.</li>
  <li>addSubview triggers layoutSubviews.</li>
  <li>Setting a view’s Frame triggers layoutSubviews (triggered when the frame changes).</li>
  <li>Scrolling a UIScrollView triggers layoutSubviews.</li>
  <li>Rotating the screen triggers the layoutSubviews event on the parent UIView.</li>
  <li>Resizing a UIView also triggers the layoutSubviews event on the parent UIView.</li>
  <li>Directly calling setLayoutSubviews.</li>
</ol>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">drawrect:</code> (the drawrect() function) is called in the following situations:</p>

<ol>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">drawrect:</code> is called after the UIViewController’s <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">loadView:</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ViewDidLoad:</code> methods.</li>
  <li>After we call <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[UIFont sizeToFit]</code>, the system automatically calls <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">drawRect:</code></li>
  <li>Setting a UIView’s contentMode or Frame immediately triggers the system to call <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">drawRect:</code></li>
  <li>Directly calling <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">setNeedsDisplay</code> to set the flag, or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">setNeedsDisplayInRect:</code>, triggers <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">drawRect:</code></li>
</ol>

<blockquote>
  <p>Knowledge expansion: when we manipulate the drawRect method, we’re actually operating on the backingStore region in memory that holds the view, for subsequent graphics rendering operations. If this is unclear, see <a href="https://www.sunyazhou.com/2017/10/UIViewRendering/">The rendering process of UIView</a>.</p>
</blockquote>

<h3 id="5-how-ui-refreshing-works">5. How UI refreshing works</h3>

<p>I’m not sure whether this question is asking about iOS’s off-screen rendering process, so let me briefly answer that.</p>

<p>iOS’s <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">MainRunloop</code> is a 60fps callback — that is, the screen is drawn once every 16.7ms (milliseconds), during which the following work must be completed:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Creation of the view’s buffer</li>
  <li>Drawing of the view’s content (if drawRect is overridden)</li>
  <li>Receiving and processing system touch events</li>
</ul>

<p>The UI graphics we see are actually the result of continuous cooperation between the CPU and GPU. After <a href="https://www.sunyazhou.com/2017/10/UIViewRendering/">the rendering process of UIView</a>, our UI continuously receives the events the system gives us.</p>

<p>Because the main thread’s runloop keeps callback-ing, our UI gets the window for refreshing. Whether rendering or handling events, it’s all the result of the runloop working non-stop. In earlier posts we learned that the main thread’s runloop is started by default, because we respond to interaction.</p>

